# Amplituhedron integration: verified boundary and open analytic frontier

The mathematical requirements suggested by this module name are stronger than
the scalar carriers previously considered here: a scalar function vanishing on
a boundary is not a canonical differential form with a logarithmic pole, and
an arbitrary scalar called an integrated volume is not an integral or a limit
theorem. This file records the precise scope of the current formalization.

The repository does contain finite algebraic results adjacent to this problem:
the twistor projection and its bracket identities, scalar reciprocal-coordinate
products, and conditional scalar BCFW cancellation. These do not construct the
amplituhedron as a domain, its canonical differential form, or an integration
operator. A subsequent formalization must first specify those objects and then prove
the boundary and convergence statements from that data. This file intentionally
declares no surrogate structures or unsupported integration theorem.
-/
